From f62917c1dad7824d593f345abee3bd8075582a8c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SorinS <ssuciu@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 22:39:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] -g, --debug-info: the debugger build apart from --debug

--debug keeps the meaning it has upstream: the overflow, range and tag
checks, and C debug information. What patches 0001-0005 added to it --
#line directives, no inlining unless an -O asks for it, one breakpoint
location to a line, the debug map, and goose run's backtraces -- now
comes with -g (or --debug-info), which also turns on --debug's checks.
A program run under --debug for its checks alone is built as before:
its C is the same as without these patches.

-g is what C compilers, rustc, swiftc and GHC call emitting debug
information, and the partner of -O, which goose already takes.

Test: --debug's C is identical to upstream's (but for the runtime's
version); -g and --debug-info give #line, the map and a frame for every
function; -g -O2 inlines and keeps #line and the map; the help and the
completion scripts name -g and --debug-info.
